--TEST--
`Core\Request\Part::saveTo` accepts a laundered destination under every spelling of its bag and still refuses the claimed one, so naming an option is not a way through the sink — `rule:http-server/a-part-is-a-file-iff-it-carries-a-filename` and `rule:core-classes/io-write-stream`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The accepting side of the boundary the case beside this one refuses at, which
// is what makes either half mean anything: three of these four calls compile,
// so what `saveTo` takes is exactly a laundered path and not merely "not this
// one". Both options are optional, either may be named alone, and the launderer
// may stand in the argument.
//
// The fourth is the same claim with `overwrite` named beside it. It is refused,
// because an option says what to do with the destination and never what the
// destination is allowed to be -- the qualifier is the parameter's and nothing
// written in the bag reaches it.
string $dir = Core\IO::temporaryDir();
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    string $safe = Core\IO::within($dir, "upload.bin");
    $part->saveTo($safe);
    $part->saveTo($safe, {overwrite: true});
    $part->saveTo(Core\IO::within($dir, "second.bin"), {overwrite: true, max: 4096});
    $part->saveTo($part->filename(), {overwrite: true});
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:18:19
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
